    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rajputs">Rajput</a> kingdom in southern Rajasthan ruled by the Guhila and Sisodia dynasty from the early 8th century to 1949.</li><li>Its fortress of Chittor fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a>, Bahadur Shah and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#akbar">Akbar</a>, and became a symbol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rajputs">Rajput</a> resistance.</li><li>Mewar accepted Mughal overlordship in 1615 and British protection in 1818, joining Rajasthan in 1948–1949.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The rulers of Mewar governed as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#diwan">diwan</a>, or chief minister, of the god Ekalinga, whom the dynasty regarded as the true king of the land.</p></aside>
    </section>

