    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#iltutmish"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iltutmish.jpg" alt="Iltutmish" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#iltutmish">Iltutmish</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Islamic_Sultanates._Delhi._Shams_al-D%C4%ABn_Iltutmish._AH_607-633_AD_1210-1235._AR_Tanka_(25mm,_11.07_g,_6h)._Sind_type.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG Coins, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mewar, known in its last centuries as Udaipur State, was the kingdom of the Guhila dynasty in the hills and valleys of southern Rajasthan. Inscriptions show Guhila chiefs in the region from the seventh century, among them the Samoli inscription of King Shiladitya dated 646. Dynastic tradition makes Bappa Rawal, who is said to have taken the great fort of Chittor from its Mori rulers in the early eighth century, the true founder, and the register’s date of 728 follows this tradition; whether Bappa is the same person as a ruler named in inscriptions is still debated. The early Guhilas ruled from Nagda and Ahar near modern Udaipur as one of several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rajputs">Rajput</a> lineages of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>, at first feudatories of the Gurjara-Pratihara, that became independent as that empire declined in the tenth century. In the thirteenth century Jaitrasimha resisted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iltutmish">Iltutmish</a> sacked Nagda in the 1220s, and Chittor became the main seat of the dynasty. In 1303 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> took Chittor after a siege of about eight months, and King Ratnasimha was killed. Around 1326 Hammir Singh, head of the Sisodia branch of the Guhilas, recovered Chittor and took the title of <em>rana</em>. His descendants, the Sisodia Maharanas, ruled Mewar without a break until 1949, one of the longest dynastic successions in Indian history.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#british-raj"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/british-raj.jpg" alt="British Raj" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#british-raj">British Raj</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_Indian_Empire_1909_Imperial_Gazetteer_of_India.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Edinburgh Geographical Institute; J. G. Bartholomew and Sons., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mewar was a land of rocky Aravalli hills, forests and modest plains with uncertain rainfall, and its farmers grew wheat, barley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maize">maize</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#millet">millets</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pulses">pulses</a>, relying heavily on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#well">wells</a> and on artificial lakes and tanks. Successive rulers built some of the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#reservoir">reservoirs</a> of pre-modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>: Lake Pichola was enlarged when Udai Singh II founded Udaipur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#british-raj">Raj</a> Singh I created Rajsamand Lake in the 1660s and 1670s, and Jai Singh built Jaisamand Lake in the late seventeenth century. Zawar, near Udaipur, was one of the earliest centres of zinc smelting in the world, and its mines continued to be worked under the Maharanas. Society was dominated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rajputs">Rajput</a> lineages that held land in return for military service, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#brahmins">Brahmins</a>, Jain merchants, who were prominent as bankers and ministers, artisans and peasants, and the Bhil communities of the hills, who served as soldiers and guides. Religion was centred on Ekalinga, a form of Shiva worshipped at the temple of Eklingji near Nagda, and in 1672 the image of Shrinathji, a form of Krishna, was brought to Nathdwara in Mewar, which became a major pilgrimage centre. The court was a patron of music and painting: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rana-kumbha">Rana Kumbha</a> is credited with treatises on music, and Mewar painting, from the Chawand <em>Ragamala</em> of 1605 to the Ramayana illustrated by Sahibdin in the 1650s, became one of the main Rajput schools.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-khanwa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-khanwa.jpg" alt="Battle of Khanwa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-khanwa">Battle of Khanwa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Babur%E2%80%99s_army_in_battle_against_the_army_of_Rana_Sanga_at.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">&quot;Mirza 'Abd al-Rahim, Khan-i khanan (Mirza 'Abd al-Rahim, Khan-i khanan)&quot;, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mewar’s armies were built from the contingents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rajputs">Rajput</a> nobles and their kinsmen, supplemented by Bhil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#archery">archers</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mercenary">mercenaries</a> and the ruler’s own troops, and its strength lay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> and in great hill fortresses such as Chittor, Kumbhalgarh and Mandalgarh. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rana-kumbha">Rana Kumbha</a> (r. 1433–1468) fought the sultans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malwa">Malwa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gujarat">Gujarat</a>, and the nine-storey Vijay Stambha, or Victory Tower, at Chittor commemorates his victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mahmud-khalji">Mahmud Khalji</a> of Malwa. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rana-sanga">Rana Sanga</a> (r. c. 1508–1528) defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ibrahim-lodi">Ibrahim Lodi</a> at Khatoli in 1517 and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mahmud-ii">Mahmud II</a> of Malwa in 1519, and in 1527 he led a coalition of Rajput and Afghan forces against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babur">Babur</a>, only to be defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-khanwa">Battle of Khanwa</a> by the Mughal combination of field guns, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#matchlock">matchlocks</a> and mobile cavalry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bahadur-shah-of-gujarat">Bahadur Shah of Gujarat</a> sacked Chittor in 1535, and in 1567–1568 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#akbar">Akbar</a> besieged and took the fortress, which was defended by Jaimal and Patta while Udai Singh II withdrew to the hills. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maharana-pratap">Maharana Pratap</a> refused submission and fought a Mughal army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#man-singh-i">Man Singh I</a> of Amber at the Battle of Haldighati on 18 June 1576; the battle was a Mughal tactical victory, but Pratap escaped and waged a long war of raids from the hills, recovering much of Mewar, though not Chittor, before his death in 1597.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#amar-singh-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amar-singh-i.jpg" alt="Amar Singh I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#amar-singh-i">Amar Singh I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raja_Ravi_Varma,_Maharana_Amar_Singh_-_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Raja Ravi Varma, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The ruler of Mewar governed in the name of the god Ekalinga, whom the dynasty regarded as the true sovereign of the land; the Maharana was his <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#diwan">diwan</a></em>, or chief minister. Power was shared with the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rajputs">Rajput</a> nobles, the heads of branches of the Sisodia clan and of allied lineages, who held estates called <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jagir">jagirs</a></em> and owed military service; tradition ranked the leading families in a first class of sixteen and a second of thirty-two. Their attendance at court, their precedence and their quarrels over land shaped politics as much as the Maharana’s decisions. Revenue came from a share of the harvest, transit dues and the income of royal lands, administered by ministers often drawn from Jain and Kayastha families. The relationship with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> was defined by the treaty of 1615, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#amar-singh-i">Amar Singh I</a> accepted Jahangir’s overlordship through Prince Khurram; Mewar was spared the marriage alliances made by other Rajput houses, the Maharana was excused personal attendance at court and his heir served in his place. In 1818 Bhim Singh accepted the protection of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#east-india-company">East India Company</a>, and Mewar became a princely state under a British political agent, with its internal administration left to the Maharana. Twentieth-century rulers introduced modern offices, courts and schools, but the state remained essentially a personal monarchy until 1948.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#maharana-pratap"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maharana-pratap.jpg" alt="Maharana Pratap" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#maharana-pratap">Maharana Pratap</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maharana_Pratap_By_Surendra_Singh_Shaktawat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Surendra Singh Shaktawat (Semliyaheera), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mewar became the emblem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rajputs">Rajput</a> resistance and honour in Indian memory. The sieges of Chittor, the self-immolation of its women in <em>jauhar</em> and the long struggle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maharana-pratap">Maharana Pratap</a> were celebrated by bards and chroniclers, and the story of Queen Padmini, first told in Malik Muhammad Jayasi’s poem <em>Padmavat</em> of 1540, attached a lasting legend to the siege of 1303, though historians regard it as fiction. James Tod, British political agent at Udaipur from 1818 to 1822, built his <em>Annals and Antiquities of Rajast’han</em> (1829–1832) around Mewar, and his romantic picture of the Rajputs influenced both British attitudes and Indian nationalists, for whom Pratap became a national hero. The poet-saint Mirabai, whose devotional songs to Krishna are sung across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>, is traditionally said to have married into the ruling house as the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rana-sanga">Rana Sanga</a>’s son Bhoj <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#british-raj">Raj</a>. Mewar’s rulers left a large architectural legacy: the forts of Chittorgarh and Kumbhalgarh are part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hill-fort">Hill Forts</a> of Rajasthan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> inscribed in 2013, and the City Palace, Jag Mandir and the Lake Palace on Lake Pichola, built in the 1740s by Jagat Singh II, made Udaipur one of the most visited cities in India. Mewar painting remains one of the principal schools of Rajput art.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#aurangzeb"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aurangzeb.jpg" alt="Aurangzeb" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aurangzeb-portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Darbarscene.jpg: Cordanrad derivative work: Rani nurmai (talk), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the treaty of 1615 Mewar was a respected but subordinate state within the Mughal system. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#british-raj">Raj</a> Singh I (r. 1652–1680) opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>’s reimposition of the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jizya">jizya</a></em> tax and supported the Rathores of Marwar in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rajputs">Rajput</a> war of 1679–1681, which ended in a compromise peace. As Mughal power collapsed in the eighteenth century, Mewar did not regain its former standing. From the 1730s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maratha-empire">Marathas</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peshwa">Peshwa</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#baji-rao-i">Baji Rao I</a> and later the Scindia and Holkar houses exacted heavy tribute, nobles feuded among themselves and the treasury was exhausted; in 1810 the young princess Krishna Kumari was poisoned to avert a war between rival suitors from Jaipur and Jodhpur, an episode that came to symbolise the state’s helplessness. The treaty of 1818 with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#east-india-company">East India Company</a> brought protection and stability at the cost of external <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, and during the uprising of 1857 Maharana Swarup Singh remained loyal to the British. At independence Maharana Bhupal Singh acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>. On 18 April 1948 Udaipur joined the United State of Rajasthan, with Bhupal Singh as its Rajpramukh, and on 30 March 1949 it merged with Jaipur, Jodhpur, Bikaner and Jaisalmer into Greater Rajasthan, where he became Maharajpramukh. The state ceased to exist, and the abolition of the princely privy purses in 1971 ended the family’s official status, though it still maintains the City Palace.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Mewar, ruled by the Guhila and Sisodia dynasty for over a millennium, was the most prestigious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rajputs">Rajput</a> kingdom, resisting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mughal-empire">Mughals</a> at Chittor, Khanwa and Haldighati, and its forts, lakes and painting, together with the legend of its resistance, made it central to Indian historical memory.</em></p>

